    "returncode": 0,
    "stdout": "Meridian Credits release \"2.0\" credit agreement (Finance-approved, current/default agreement as of trial-tc2), permanently replacing release 1.0 as the current agreement for every supplied charge set regardless of service dates:\n\n- Each charge line's uncapped credit = floor(charge_cents * 15 / 100), i.e. 15% of charge_cents rounded down to integer cents.\n- The credit cap is 3000 cents, shared by all lines with the same account_id ACROSS ITS SUBSCRIPTIONS (unlike release 1.0, which capped per (account_id, subscription_id) pair). Distinct account_id groups have independent caps.\n- Within a cap group, credit is assigned to lines in priority order: largest charge_cents first; ties broken by increasing line_id (Unicode code-point order). This priority rule (largest-charge-first) differs from release 1.0's earliest-service_on-first rule. Each line gets its uncapped credit up to the group's remaining cap; later lines in priority order get only what remains, or zero.\n- total credit = sum of assigned line credits; a line's amount_due_cents = charge_cents - assigned credit_cents; total due = total charges - total credit.\n- No line is excluded by service_on.\n- Requests to \"quote\" and \"total\" with an omitted \"release\" now use \"2.0\" (previously defaulted to \"1.0\"). Explicit release \"2.0\" behaves the same as omitted. Explicit release \"1.0\" continues to use the original release 1.0 agreement (see meridian-credits/release-1.0-agreement) unchanged. Request/response shapes are unchanged; the response \"release\" field identifies the agreement actually used.\n- Versions are cumulative: release 1.0 remains supported via explicit \"release\":\"1.0\" so public suites naming it stay valid.\n\nSource: issue trial-tc2 description (Finance-approved release 2.0 agreement text), verified via public_tests/release_2_quote_total.json and the issue's worked example (charge_cents 19999 -> credit_cents 2999, amount_due_cents 17000), plus manual verification of the account-wide cap-sharing and largest-charge-first priority ordering across multiple subscriptions under one account.\n",
